    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_036275316676c210d6632dee0a0c9598efa61cca3fb0abf7f6d4508870d4c272",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Replacing a coalesced moment power by a product at independently shifted arguments is a reversible representation change at the level stated here: it exposes the product's parameterized structure, supports parallel continuous t-aspect, conductor-ordered family, and compact-group averages, and recovers the original moments by sending the shifts to zero. The gain is a common comparison interface; the coalesced form forgets the distinctions among the shifted factors. These definitions alone do not establish a mean-value conjecture, and the admissible class of 'suitable' weights is intentionally left open beyond an example.",
      "parent_sources": [
