    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_7b7939005f44db182fe562afb78bec41d42dcfabe0ecb6b5ab73e12510496883",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For unitary A, the characteristic polynomial is a controlled analogue of an L-function: it preserves an expansion, reciprocal functional equation, critical locus, zero-density scale, critical value, and a functional-equation decomposition, while its lack of an Euler product marks arithmetic local data as information the model does not carry. Matching matrix size N to the L-function log conductor therefore matches local zero density rather than merely matching notation. The coefficient decomposition has two displayed pieces for odd N, but an even N matrix has an additional central term. Determinant or root-number behavior can suggest a symmetry class, yet moments or low-lying-zero density still have to determine it.",
      "parent_sources": [
